+# Remaining Stage 7 obligation
+
+The universal first-source assertion is not proved. Do not mark Stage7 complete
+or begin a witness-guaranteed Stage8 algorithm on the strength of this package.
+
+Completed:
+- exact mixed-order coefficient section with integer multiplicities and carries;
+- optimal bound within that finite subgroup-chain class, including nonunit steps;
+- original hard-prime packets that improve on the old unit-width test;
+- sharp last-capacity failure in actual original prime packets;
+- an upward, source-changing plus-factor relation with complete inverse fibres;
+- the p67369 trace repaired at a'=a+1, and an infinite reduced prime family.
+
+The main positive arithmetic example specifically passes through the previously
+proved obstruction to universal nonincrease of a. Its source u4067 maps to the
+E word674 at a16850. Do not change its output channel or confuse its cofactor
+with its residual. The fixed plus-factor fibre at p97 is entirely unoccupied
+in both full channels, so that fibre relation alone is not a universal selector.
+
+The chain lower bound and the old unit-width bound are complementary. D3 with
+two length-two unit intervals is positive but has no valid chain. D9 with two
+length-two intervals of steps3,1 generates the group but still has absent bins.
+Neither subgroup generation nor an unconstrained quotient representative may
+replace the actual digit lengths.
+
+Within the complete p<=10000 original replay, the chain is positive on four
+coarse parts and the old unit bound on five; there is no chain-only part in that
+small census. The strict improvement is proved and exhibited on the separately
+certified larger original packets. No extra prime coverage is inferred from
+those selected positive examples.
+
+The next necessary theorem must force an initial trace/part with an occupied
+coefficient, or link different original shell fibres so that a full gate is
+unavoidable. A fixed-C component or an arithmetic boundary packet may be empty.
+No well-founded global termination measure has been proved by the isolated
+one-shell upward transformation.
